#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""skill-log — a local SQLite log of skill invocations, problems tied to them.

One row per skill invocation, in ~/.local/state/skill-log/skills.db; problem
rows reference the invocation that produced them. Skills append silently at
the end of their work (boilerplate: the create-skill skill's
references/usage-log.md). A clean run writes a problem-less row — the
invocation count is the denominator of the problems/invocation rate. A
row is marked resolved by a later, separate step: `resolve` stamps the
commit that carries the fix and the time it was resolved, so the log
can show which invocations are still open. The commit is a post-commit
step — the squash merge's hash is the one the fix ships in — so the
local db is updated after the merge, not at the skill's own commit.

  skill-log add --skill <name> [--parent <id>] [--problem "..."] ... [--t3-thread <id>]
  skill-log show [--skill <name>] [--machine <host>] [--unresolved] [--last N]
  skill-log stats [--skill <name>] [--by-model]
  skill-log resolve --id <invocation> --fix <commit> [--dry-run]
  skill-log resolve-merged [--since <rev>] [--dry-run] [--quiet]
  skill-log fix --id <invocation>... | --thread <id> [--check]
  skill-log sync --from <host> [--remote-db <path>]

Every command accepts --db <path>, defaulting to that file above.
add writes; show/stats read and never insert rows — though both converge
an old db's schema on first touch (one ALTER per missing column; a
read-only --db touches nothing). Date, model, project, opencode thread id,
worktree, and machine are auto-detected; --t3-thread and the rest are
overridable as flags. A dispatched subagent's row passes --parent — the T3
thread id of the run that spawned it — so a dispatch tree's rows coalesce
under one thread for dedupe. Model detect happens at add time against the
local opencode session store for the row's session id — a session stored on
another machine can't be read from here, so it lands blank unless --model
overrides it.

resolve-merged closes the loop on its own: it reads the merged change for
the invocations it resolves — a `.skill-log/fixes/` report file when the
checkout has one, the commit-body `Skill-log-Fix:` trailer otherwise — and
stamps each named row with that commit's hash. It is idempotent, so a git
post-merge hook can run it after every pull, which is how a merged fix
becomes a resolved row without anyone remembering. `fix` writes the report
file: one `machine#id` per line, validated at authoring time and committed
with the change, so a squash merge cannot lose or reword it.

sync pulls another machine's log over SSH and merges its rows into this db,
so one machine can read every host's rows at once. The log is per-machine
and a row's id is only unique inside the db that wrote it, so a merged row
keeps its owner: machine is the row's host, origin_id is its id there, and
that pair is the merge's dedupe key. Re-running merges nothing twice and
takes the owner's fix/resolved_at forward; rows this db wrote are never
touched. A host renamed between a short name and its FQDN keeps the spelling
the db already holds, so a rename neither lands as a second machine nor
strands the markers written under the old spelling. The one-way direction is
deliberate: the merge runs on the machine that can reach both dbs (the
desktop pulling a VPS), and the remote is only read — its rows are never
modified, though SQLite does create its -shm/-wal sidecars beside a WAL db,
so the remote log needs a writable directory.
"""

